ent his  misplaced confidence and go on during  the cell games or even his decision to  fight Android 19 in the first place  comments kept pouring in touting example  after example however and unsurprisingly  a significant amount of discussion and  arguing stemmed from the various  responses to this tweet of mine largely  because I believe now having looked into  his callin Goku overconfident would be  at best a very low resolution  observation of his character allow me to  explain what I mean overconfidence as  it's defined is a belief in one's own  abilities that exceed the parameters of  reality in other words one who is  overconfident has no reason at all to be  interestingly a perfect example of this  could be observed with Vegeta who during  the cell saga allowed Sal to reach his  perfect form simply because Vegeta  believed no one could ever be as  powerful as he was a belief in his own  abilities that exceeded the realms of  reality resulting in reality hitting him  hard so how does this apply or not apply  to the story of Goku's adult life well  from my experience reading about Goku  it's true that he's naive innocent  selfish honest and tenacious but  overconfidence seems like a stretch in a  lot of ways to me let's look at some  examples for the very same reason Goku  was excited to fight Frieza while  traveling to Namak he allows Frieza to  reach max power here in this instance  Goku is aware of what he's doing what  the consequences are and he's ready to  face them in this instance he's  confident that he can defeat Frieza this  might very well have been chosen through  a selfish self-indulgent path to revenge  but he wasn't overconfident  overconfidence requires confidence that  is unwarranted  in order for Goku to be overconfident  his confidence in this scene would have  to be missed  placed and as I'm sure you're aware it  wasn't he was confident that he could  defeat Frieza and he did with relative  ease all things considered in this case  his confidence wasn't misplaced  similarly his assertion to Vegeta that  he was stronger than him in the cell  saga wasn't cockiness or overconfidence  either but a statement of fact and  observation of reality he was in fact  stronger than Vegeta during that time  but regardless above all else what Goku  really wants is to challenge himself  against the strongest opponents he can  thanks to Master Roshi's teachings in  early Dragonball he trains and fights  under the philosophy that there will  always be someone stronger around the  next corner there's a humbleness in that  mindset he doesn't presuppose that he's  invincible he expects there to be  someone better and these encounters  translate into opportunities for Goku to  always push himself to greater and  greater heights  Goku does let his guard down allowing  his opponent the best chance to defeat  him but it's inaccurate I think to say  that this is as a result of  overconfidence quite the opposite  what he lets his guard down he's not  necessarily confident that he will get  away with it because anything that would  feel like he would get away with it  would be boring to him he wants to  always ride the razor's edge of battle  pushing closer and closer to his limits  and going beyond his comfort and  confidence zone he specifically seeks  circumstances where he isn't confident  in his abilities that's where the fun is  for Goku in a fight and that's why he  said things like he's scared but also  excited the desire to test himself  against a strong opponent has always  overpowered his fear to run  this particular scene with Android 19  was yet another instance that came up  time and again in the comments section  of that tweet I sent out I'm not sure  what about this demonstrates co-could  being copier overconfident  Goku sort of chooses to fight the  Android because he's the most capable at  the time and he's the one they're after  he's also under the impression that  Vegeta will show up sooner or later as  backup as said manga in addition to this  he also doesn't know what's happening to  his body you can label him as foolish or  forgetful here but not overconfident in  these high-pressure instances he isn't  confident that he'll win but he is  confident in his abilities and that's  what he's searching for anytime he finds  himself up against tough odds he pushes  himself and doesn't quit unless  completely outmatched and even saying  that he has given up and not even  attempted to fight in some cases because  he knew he was outmatched  I do give up I've given all I've got  lord beerus but there's still no way I  can beat you for instance or if you want  an even earlier example check out this  one there's no way that we can defeat a  power like cell in a fight right now  not exactly the move you'd see from  someone who would be labeled as  overconfident however the most popular  example of Goku being overconfident in  the series within my little Twitter  thread was his decision to pit Gohan up  against perfect cell all the while  throwing a sense repeat itself to even  up the odds in his mind and what's  interesting about that specific scene  with Gohan is that Goku was technically  correct his confidence in what Gohan was  capable of wasn't misplaced what he  didn't count on was that he understood  that better than Gohan did that  particular circumstance is way more  nuanced than simple overconfidence and I  think a better word to describe that  decision is naivety naivety is described  as the lack of experience wisdom or  judgment sometimes out of innocence and  that perfectly describes this particular  misunderstanding between Gohan and Goku  and let's make no bones about this this  is Goku's flaw it's not over confidence  in himself it's that he naively  presupposes that everyone be they his  friends or his enemies treaty fight with  the same honor as he does it's for this  very reason he's constantly taken aback  during instances where his opponent  treats their  fight like anything other than a fair  contest whether it be Vegeta's sudden  decision to blow up the earth or Gohan  not wanting to go all-out in the fight  Goku is always caught off guard by  someone else's worldview in these  high-pressure circumstances allowing  cell a sense of be letting Vegeta and  Frieza go these are all examples of his  selfishness and/or naivety shining  through to label it overconfidence is  inaccurate  despite Goku and Gohan dynamic in the  cell saga we see in resurrection F isn't  talking about Goku's overconfidence in  another it's over confidence in himself  criticizing that he constantly lets his  guard down  which he does but as I explained it's  not that he is overconfident or  overestimate his abilities he lowers his  guard and doesn't go all-out because he  selfishly wants to get more out of the  fight if he thought that his opponent  was way below him and he overestimated  his own abilities he'd be bored and  that's just not how Goku operates in  resurrection F Wiest tries to  demonstrate to goku that he let his  guard down too often by punching him in  his ribs as he's walking through the  garden of beerus his planet after the  sparring session had been brought to a  close this doesn't demonstrate anything  in order for Goku to have his guard up  in this non-combative scenario he would  either need to be consciously on guard  24/7 which is impractical and exactly  the floor Vegeta has or he would need to  have ultra instinct which is the goal  wheezes training them towards so I don't  understand how this could be an  indication that he's anything other than  a weaker fired than Wiese it doesn't  demonstrate anything to further wheezes  point about him being overconfident I  mean if we really want to get into it  overconfidence is much more accurately a  trait of Vegeta's than Goku s-- he has  in the past time and again legitimately  brought about his own demise due to this  overconfidence in his abilities  I'm saying all of this while being fully  aware that akira toriyama wrote  resurrection F because this whole essay  isn't to say I think giving Goku a  shortcoming in the film was a bad idea  I actually think it's a wonderful and  interesting idea if handled correctly  characters change archetypes all the  time and for Goku to shift from a flat  arc character into a positive one is a  really fascinating development for his  character if they handle it correctly  thereafter which they didn't  as soon as resurrection have concluded  Goku shortcomings from that story  suddenly disappear never to be seen  again despite Goku paying for that  shortcoming in that very film when you  give a character a negative trait to  overcome the journey ought to be usually  centered around consequences of their  internal conflict in rejecting this  negative trait of theirs suddenly not  having it isn't development its sudden  change development is gradual not sudden  which to me is a shame because if Goku  attained ultra instinct for a genuine  growth and development reason rather  than just simply getting it it would  have given an already very cool scene  and emotional weight behind it that we  don't normally see with Goku in these  circumstances it would have made it a  very unique moment for Goku specifically  in the series and that's just it in a  nutshell  instead of Arc's flowing into each other  as consistently and cohesively as the  original series did many arcs have  beginning points and end points things  suddenly happen because plot needs to  happen and characters are given things  to work on that really if you think  about it long enough don't make a lot of  sense for the character in the entire  series the closest instance I could find  to adults Goku actually having misplaced  confidence in himself was early in the  freezer fight he never believed the  fight would be easy but he wasn't  prepared for how monstrous freezer  really was however to paint this as  overconfidence I think is a stretch and  even if you consider it full-blown  overconfidence to consider this worn  instance grounds to label Goku as a  definitive overconfident character is I  think disingenuous but at the end of the  day writing is still one of the most  difficult things when it comes to art  Dragon Ball super captured my  imagination and for that  alone I would label it a success but  just because I like and enjoy something  doesn't mean I have to turn a blind eye  instead of questioning things for me  that's one of the more fun aspects of  watching a TV show figuring out how it's  written and where it doesn't make sense  and that really was the impetus for this  entire anatomy of anime series I'm so  incredibly thankful that so many of you  have decided to share your mornings  afternoons and evenings with me over  this last year I'm truly humbled and  will continue to try my best to improve  but until then let me know what you  think about wheeshes comments towards  Goku down below do you think he's  overconfident am i right am I wrong let  me know but that will do it for this  week I've been totally not mark thank  you so much for watching  [Music]
 

